# Normalise stored line endings so that a diff never turns into a whole-file
# rewrite because two contributors are on different platforms.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must be checked out with LF on every platform. A CRLF line
# ending after the shebang is part of the interpreter path, so a script checked
# out with CRLF on Windows fails to run at all. This is correctness, not style.
*.sh text eol=lf

# uv writes the lockfile with LF on every platform. Without this, a Windows
# checkout receives CRLF and the next `uv lock` rewrites the whole file, which
# turns a no-op into a diff.
uv.lock text eol=lf

# A workflow's `run:` blocks are shell scripts that a Linux runner executes, and
# a CRLF line ending inside one becomes a literal carriage return in the command
# — the same failure `*.sh` above is protected from, in a file that does not look
# like a script. `text=auto` already normalises what is stored; this pins what is
# checked out, so a runner cannot receive CRLF whatever the checkout did.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf

# The Application procedure records the SHA-256 of each manifest it applies. With
# LF on every platform, the recorded digest names the committed bytes whatever the
# checkout did.
infra/argocd/*.yaml text eol=lf
.github/workflows/*.yaml text eol=lf

# The chart hashes its own rendered configuration into a pod annotation
# (`inferops.io/configuration-checksum`), so the bytes of every template and
# values file are part of the output. A CRLF checkout renders a different
# checksum from an LF one, and the committed renders under ci/rendered/ can
# only match one of them. Pinning the checkout makes the render the same on
# every platform: before this, the committed renders carried checksums derived
# from a Windows working tree, and the first hosted Linux run failed both.
charts/** text eol=lf

# The proof dashboard is generated from the claim and evidence register, and the
# generator pins its own newlines on every platform: `--write` opens the file with
# `newline="\n"` and `--page` reconfigures the stream it prints to, so the same
# register produces the same bytes wherever it runs. Pinning the checkout is the
# other half — without it a Windows working tree acquires CRLF that the next
# `--write` immediately undoes, which turns a no-op into a whole-file diff.
docs/proof/dashboard.md text eol=lf

# The V1 evidence index is generated the same way, from the register and the
# normalization ledger, with its newlines pinned by `--write`, and a test compares
# the committed file with what the generator produces. Pinned for the same reason.
docs/proof/v1-evidence-index.v1alpha1.json text eol=lf

# The generated Prometheus rule files are compared byte for byte against what
# `python -m tools.inference_alerts --rules` prints, and a collector's promtool
# reads them out of a Linux container. `text=auto` already normalises what is
# stored; this pins what is checked out, so neither comparison depends on which
# platform wrote the file.
deploy/prometheus/*.yaml text eol=lf

# The generated Helm values and the generated release under this directory are
# compared byte for byte with what the renderer writes, which is LF on every
# platform, and a generated file's digest is the digest of its bytes. Pinned so a
# Windows checkout cannot turn a golden file into a CRLF copy that no render matches.
tests/domain/fixtures/helm-values/** text eol=lf

# An E01 run's evidence is checked again from its bytes: each rendered file is
# compared with the other render and with the digest the release beside it records,
# and the run's manifest records the SHA-256 of every file it wrote, as written, LF
# on every platform. Pinned so a Windows checkout cannot turn the evidence into CRLF
# copies whose digests no longer match the run that wrote them.
docs/proof/experiments/v2-e01/runs/** text eol=lf

# The Git desired state holds generated releases, each compared byte for byte with
# what its declared sources derive, and each release records the SHA-256 of its
# values file's bytes. The renderer writes LF on every platform. Pinned so a
# Windows checkout cannot turn the desired state into CRLF copies that no render
# matches.
gitops/** text eol=lf
# The run record of the reconciliation observation states the SHA-256 of each
# record and of the driver, so the bytes of these files are the same on every
# host.
docs/proof/environment/v2-s3-003-pr2-reconciliation-observation-* text eol=lf

# The single-runtime baseline profile is a generated release and a comparison
# record. Each is compared byte for byte with what the tool derives, and the
# release records the SHA-256 of its values file's bytes. Pinned so a Windows
# checkout cannot turn either into a CRLF copy that no derivation matches.
tests/domain/fixtures/experiment-profiles/** text eol=lf
